  "markdown": "# Capacity review — Astronomy Shop on Kubernetes — history\n\nHistory 13 Oct 2026 16:45 – 13 Oct 2026 17:23 UTC, busiest moment 164.5 req/s (requests a second). SLO, the target: p99 ≤ 400 ms (99% of requests answered within it), errors ≤ 2.0%; time out of SLO is the minutes that missed it.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Risk:** **Medium.** Traffic holds to 2×, but losing **bnk8s-worker** at the busiest moment fails 49,700 more requests and 7.5 min out of SLO.\n- **Headroom:** 391.4 req/s within SLO, 2.38× the busiest moment (164.5 req/s); frontend saturates first.\n- **Cost:** about **$576/month** as run ($6,912 a year), on 2 nodes 30% full at the busiest moment; a commitment on the always-on floor saves up to $312/month.\n- **Do this:** run frontend × 4, checkout × 2, payment × 2, email × 2, shipping × 2, quote × 2, currency × 2, recommendation × 2, ad × 2, cart × 2, valkey-cart × 2, flagd × 2, product-catalog × 2 spread over the nodes, so losing one leaves a replica of each — no added cost (it fits on the nodes already running). Run `verify_change` on it before it ships.\n- **Trust:** 12 of 14 services fit the history well; not explained: recommendation, jaeger — numbers that rest on them are guesses. Its predictions were not checked on held-back history (see calibrate_model's warnings).\n\n## Headroom\n\nWithin SLO up to **391.4 req/s** — 2.38× the busiest moment; frontend saturates first.\n\n- **The recorded day, replayed:** holds\n- **1.5× today's traffic:** holds (0.5 min out of SLO, within the half minute allowed)\n- **2× today's traffic:** holds (0.5 min out of SLO, within the half minute allowed)\n\n## Losing a node at the busiest moment\n\nLosing **bnk8s-worker** fails 49,700 more requests and 7.5 min out of SLO: checkout, payment, email, shipping, quote, currency, recommendation have no replica left.\n\nSingle points of failure (every replica on one node): ad, checkout, payment, email, shipping, quote, cart, valkey-cart, currency, flagd, product-catalog, recommendation, jaeger, otel-collector.\n\nFix, backtested: frontend → 4, checkout → 2, payment → 2, email → 2, shipping → 2, quote → 2, currency → 2, recommendation → 2, ad → 2, cart → 2, valkey-cart → 2, flagd → 2, product-catalog → 2, spread over the nodes — survives every node, +$0/month.\n\n## Limits that bind\n\nThrottled services are slowed by their CPU limit (the CPU quota) before their CPU looks busy: what they serve per replica is capped below what the limit suggests.\n\n- **frontend** is held back by its CPU quota: throttling takes over at 66% of its 0.5-core limit (134.1 req/s per replica)\n- **email** is held back by its CPU quota: throttling takes over at 30% of its 0.25-core limit (58.6 req/s per replica)\n- **email** leaks: its memory grows +9.6 MiB per 1,000 requests it serves, reaching its 150 MiB limit after about 8,132 requests per container — then it is OOM-killed and restarts. A busier day gets there sooner; a restart before then, a higher limit or fixing the leak avoids it\n\n## Node pool\n\n2 nodes of 11 cores and 7.7 GiB, a fixed pool. At the busiest moment every pod's requests fill **30%** of it.\n\nIt can't shrink: 2 nodes is the fewest that hold the busiest moment's requests with one node to spare for losing one.\n\n## Commitment\n\nThe history never ran fewer than **2 nodes** ($576/month on demand): capacity that is always on, which a commitment discounts. 1-year reserved (no upfront) saves about **$192/month**; 3-year reserved (no upfront) saves about **$312/month**.\n\nNodes priced as EC2 m6i on demand; reserved m6i is 33% / 54% off (no upfront). Commit to the floor, not the peak: what runs above it stays on demand.\n\nEvery number is a replay of the recorded history through the calibrated model; `verify_change` the one you ship for its full evidence.\n"
}

